.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKM 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.33" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekm \- manage Euclid encryption keys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B ekm
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ekm
module talks to the Euclid gateway's ekm service to create, list,
revoke and delete encryption keys, and to encrypt/decrypt data with
them. Most
.B ekm
commands send a single JSON request to the gateway
(\fB\-\-endpoint\fR, default \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR) and print the
JSON response on success; the
.B encrypt
and
.B decrypt
actions instead send and receive raw bytes - see
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-encrypt (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-decrypt (1)
for details.
.PP
All
.B ekm
commands require the caller to be authenticated; see
.B AUTHENTICATION
below for how the session used to sign these requests is obtained.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-key
Creates a new key with a given algorithm and length. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-create-key (1).
.TP
.B list-keys
Lists existing keys, paged and sorted. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-keys (1).
.TP
.B delete-key
Schedules a key for permanent deletion after a grace period (default
7 days); decryption stays available until then, but encryption is
blocked immediately. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-key (1).
.TP
.B revoke-key
Revokes a key: blocks encryption permanently while leaving decryption
untouched, without scheduling the key for removal. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-revoke-key (1).
.TP
.B encrypt
Encrypts a file or stdin with a key, writing the ciphertext to a file
or stdout. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-encrypt (1).
.TP
.B decrypt
Decrypts a file or stdin with a key, writing the plaintext to a file
or stdout. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-decrypt (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I ekm
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a JSON configuration file providing defaults for action
options (currently used by
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1)
only). Defaults to
.IR /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid.json ;
silently ignored if the file doesn't exist.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature " " \fISCHEME\fR
Signature scheme used for signed service calls: \fIsigv4\fR (the
default) or \fIrfc9421\fR for RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures. Both
prove the request with the same access key; RFC 9421 puts the
signature in \fISignature\fR/\fISignature\-Input\fR headers and binds
the body through \fIContent\-Digest\fR (RFC 9530) instead of using
AWS's Authorization header. Overrides
.I euclid.cli.signature
from the configuration file. Ignored for
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1)
commands, which authenticate with the session's bearer token.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the euclid-cli version and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B ekm
commands are authenticated using the session created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials .
Like
.BR eqs (1)
and
.BR esm (1)
requests, they are signed with SigV4 using the stored access key ID and
secret if present, falling back to the bearer token otherwise.
.PP
If the server rejects a request with
.IR "HTTP 401" ,
run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, the request could not be sent, or the server
rejected it (missing/expired session, invalid input, etc.). Details are
printed to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
JSON file holding the current session's bearer token and/or SigV4
access key, created by
.B euclid-cli eam login
and read by every
.B ekm
action.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a key, list keys, encrypt/decrypt a file, revoke the key, and
schedule it for deletion:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ekm create-key \e
    --algorithm aes-gcm --length 256

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ekm list-keys

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ekm encrypt \e
    --key-id 3f1c2b7a-... --input plain.txt --output plain.enc

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ekm decrypt \e
    --key-id 3f1c2b7a-... --input plain.enc --output plain.txt

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ekm revoke-key \e
    --key ern:ekm:eu-central-1:000000000000:key:3f1c2b7a-...

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ekm delete-key \e
    --key-id 3f1c2b7a-...
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
